pinneapple_mcp.doc:
PINNeAPPle as an MCP server: solve, benchmark and validate physics problems from any coding agent.

    pip install "pinneapple[mcp]"
    pinneapple-mcp                       # stdio server; or: python -m pinneapple_mcp

The tools (``pinneapple_mcp.tools``) are plain functions with JSON inputs and outputs, usable without MCP:

* ``list_methods`` / ``describe_method``: solve methods and the method catalog with its validation status;
* ``list_presets``: problem presets;
* ``solve``: a problem (preset or JSON spec) by a method, with physics checks and a trust card (a job);
* ``trust_card``: physics checks of a field given inline or in a file (residual, boundary conditions,
  conservation, reference, applicability);
* ``benchmark``: several methods on one problem with the same training budget (a job);
* ``job_status`` / ``list_jobs`` / ``cancel_job``: long work runs as a job in a separate process;
* ``capabilities``: what this installation offers (optional APIs, limits, working directory).

Safe defaults (``pinneapple_mcp.config``): every result goes to a working directory and its paths are returned;
jobs run in a child process with a wall-time limit, a memory limit and the network switched off; files are read
only from allowed roots. ``import pinneapple_mcp`` does not import the MCP SDK.

pinneapple_analysis.inverse_problems.pre.doc:
Ready-made inverse problems with known answers, and models described as JSON (for apps and agents).

Every preset's measurements are *synthetic*: the forward model at the stated true parameters plus Gaussian noise
from a fixed seed, so any estimate can be checked against the truth (``Parameter.truth``). The heat cases are the
Inverse Heat Lab's (pin fin and heat-spreader plate), plus a fin with zone-wise convection (a field), an unknown
base temperature (a boundary condition), a cooling curve and a reaction-kinetics case.

``model_from_spec`` builds a model from JSON::

    {"type": "pin_fin", "options": {"d_mm": 5, "length_mm": 50}}
    {"type": "expression", "expressions": {"T": "T_air + (T0 - T_air) * exp(-k * t)"}, "inputs": ["t"],
     "parameters": [{"name": "k", "value": 0.01, "lower": 1e-5, "upper": 1}, ...]}
    {"type": "table", "rows": [{...}, ...], "parameters": ["h"], "inputs": ["x_mm"], "output": "T"}

pinneapple_analysis.study_models.doc:
One model interface for uncertainty and sensitivity studies: ``predict(X) -> (n, m)`` with input/output names,
units and validity range, whatever the model is.

- :meth:`StudyModel.from_zoo`: a ``pp.zoo`` model (torch); exposes the physical-inputs torch module for automatic
  differentiation, the ensemble spread when the model has one, its validity range and its reference dataset splits;
- :meth:`StudyModel.from_onnx`: an ONNX graph (no Python code from the user is run): one ``(n, d)`` float input,
  outputs concatenated in graph order; needs ``onnxruntime``;
- :meth:`StudyModel.from_function`: any vectorised Python callable (the built-in benchmark functions, a closed-form
  model, a solver wrapper);
- :func:`builtin`: the analytic benchmarks of :mod:`pinneapple_analysis.sensitivity.testfunctions`.

pinneapple_analysis.surrogate.splits.doc:
Train / validation / held-out test / distribution-shift splits for surrogate studies.

The distribution-shift split is carved out first and never seen in training, so its error is a measured
extrapolation error, not an estimate:

``"group"``     whole groups held out (a geometry, a case, a machine never seen in training);
``"edge"``      the samples farthest from the centre of the input space (normalised distance), i.e. the boundary of
                the design space, while training covers the interior;
``{"column": name, "side": "high" | "low"}``  the top or bottom of one input (extrapolation along that parameter);
``"auto"``      ``data.meta["shift"]`` when the dataset recommends one, else ``"group"`` when the data has groups,
                else ``"edge"``; ``None`` for no shift split.

The rest is split at random into train, validation (model selection and early stopping only) and the held-out test.

pinneapple_analysis.uncertainty.distribu.doc:
Uncertain inputs of a study: one distribution per parameter, sampled by Monte Carlo, Latin hypercube or Sobol.

A parameter is a dict (or anything with ``to_dict()``) such as::

    {"name": "k", "dist": "uniform", "low": 150, "high": 400, "unit": "W/(m K)"}
    {"name": "T", "dist": "normal", "mean": 25, "std": 2}
    {"name": "V", "dist": "triangular", "low": 0.5, "mode": 2, "high": 6}
    {"name": "h", "dist": "lognormal", "median": 30, "gsd": 1.2}       # geometric standard deviation
    {"name": "t", "dist": "truncnormal", "mean": 2, "std": 0.3, "low": 1.5, "high": 2.5}

or a ``{name: (low, high)}`` mapping (uniform on each range). Every distribution is sampled through its inverse CDF
from a point of the unit hypercube, so the same design (QMC, LHS, Saltelli, Morris, polynomial chaos) serves every
distribution. Used by :mod:`pinneapple_analysis.sensitivity` and the propagation in
:func:`pinneapple_analysis.uncertainty.tolerance.propagate_space`.

pinneapple_analysis.validation.fault_inj.doc:
The detection matrix: every check against every fault and magnitude, and against clean cases.

``run_fault_injection(problem)`` draws correct cases from the problem's verified solver (with the variation a
threshold must tolerate: coefficient contrast, feature size, amplitude, solver resolution), injects every fault at
every magnitude and records each check's value. From the values:

* the detection rate of each check (and of all checks together) per fault and magnitude;
* the false-alarm rate of each check on the clean cases;
* the minimal magnitude each check detects (detection rate >= ``min_rate``, default 90 %);
* the ROC AUC of each check per fault and magnitude (clean values against faulty values);
* the true error each fault causes (relative L2 against the correct field).

The report renders as Markdown (the "check power" section of a validation report) and as JSON.

pinneapple_autolab.lab.doc:
The autonomous physics lab: plan -> build -> simulate -> learn -> optimise -> verify -> deliver, on any simulator.

    lab = AutonomousLab(mission, simulator, "runs/aero")
    lab.run()                                   # the whole scientific cycle, deterministic
    lab.summary()                               # what was simulated, learned, chosen and verified, with the numbers

Every step is also a method an agent can call on its own (``plan``, ``simulate``, ``learn``, ``optimize``,
``assess``, ``choose_next``, ``verify``); each one writes an event to the ledger, and only the ledger's runs are
evidence. The lab enforces the rules a careless optimiser breaks:

* no design is simulated before its geometry and boundary conditions pass the simulator's checks;
* the operating conditions of the mission are fixed (the simulator owns them; nothing here can change them);
* no simulation beyond the budget (``BudgetExhaustedError``), with ``reserve_verify`` runs kept for the end;
* a relative limit ("keep the baseline's downforce") is only applied once the baseline was simulated;
* the error of the surrogate is measured on designs it had not seen (cross-validation, and each acquisition run's
  prediction recorded *before* the run);
* the recommendation is a simulated design, never a prediction; designs only predicted are labelled as such.

pinneapple_data.cae.doc:
CAE data layer: read meshes and results from OpenFOAM, CalculiX/Abaqus, Gmsh, VTK, STL and point tables into one
``Mesh`` type; mesh quality (checkMesh-equivalent finite-volume metrics, element shape metrics, surface checks);
per-cell Courant number and wall distance.

pinneapple_design.fitmesh.doc:
Fit-for-purpose geometry and mesh: from a problem description to checked CAD, planned meshes and one report.

1. read the problem (physics, fluid, speed, size, purpose, quantities) and ask for what is missing;
2. suggest reference geometries from a catalogue with their published sources;
3. suggest the parameters to vary and their ranges, and sample the variants (Latin hypercube, base first);
4. build STEP and STL per variant with validity, volume, tessellation and reference-object checks;
5. plan the mesh for the purpose (screening / design / verification): method, sizes, prism layers from y+,
   domain, cell and cost estimate, and the mesh family for the grid-convergence index;
6. generate the meshes (Gmsh, snappyHexMesh) and measure their quality;
7. write one report with a verdict per stage and the evidence behind it.

Optional language-model help (local via Ollama, or hosted) at every step through :class:`LLMAssist`.

pinneapple_design.fitmesh.api.doc:
HTTP API over :class:`FitMeshSession`: one session per study, one call per step, the files and the report.

from pinneapple_design.fitmesh.api import create_app
app = create_app("fitmesh_runs/")          # uvicorn module:app

POST /sessions                 {"text": "...", "llm": {"provider": "ollama", "model": "qwen2.5:7b"}}  (llm optional)
GET  /sessions/{id}            current step, questions and options
POST /sessions/{id}/answer     {"accuracy": "design", "speed": 20}
POST /sessions/{id}/geometry   {"key": "pipe_bend_90"}  or {"key": "freeform", "description": "..."}
POST /sessions/{id}/parameters {"vary": {"bend_ratio": [1, 3]}, "n": 4}   (omit vary for the suggestion)
POST /sessions/{id}/cad
POST /sessions/{id}/plan       {"budget_cells": 5e6}
POST /sessions/{id}/meshes     {"variants": [0], "max_cells": 3e6, "procs": 4}
POST /sessions/{id}/results    {"quantity": "pressure drop", "values": [f1, f2, f3], "variant": 0}
GET  /sessions/{id}/report     JSON report (?format=html for the page)
GET  /sessions/{id}/files/{path}  any file of the study (STEP, STL, .msh, .inp, report)

pinneapple_design.fitmesh.cad.doc:
Build the CAD variants, export STEP and STL, and check each one before it is meshed.

Checks per variant (each a row with value, limit and verdict):

- parameters inside the template's validity limits;
- the solid is valid (OpenCascade BRep check) and closed;
- volume against the exact formula of the template (or its bound);
- the STL is watertight and its volume matches the solid within the tessellation tolerance for the purpose;
- the bounding box matches the parameters;
- comparison with each published reference object: relative difference per shared dimension, and whether the
  variant is the reference, inside the range the reference covered, or an extrapolation.

pinneapple_design.fitmesh.catalog.doc:
Parametric geometry templates with the references that make them checkable.

Each template is a reference geometry from the literature (or a textbook case with a closed-form answer), with:

- the physics it is meant for and the words that point to it in a problem description;
- its parameters, each with the value of the reference object, a typical study range and hard validity limits;
- the published reference objects it can be compared with (dimensions and what was measured on them);
- a CadQuery builder, the exact volume where it has one, the smallest feature (which drives the mesh) and the
  reference length (which defines the Reynolds number or the element size).

All lengths are in metres and angles in degrees.

pinneapple_design.fitmesh.llm.doc:
Optional language-model help for every step, with a local model if you like (Ollama via ``pinneapple_llm``).

The model proposes; PINNeAPPle checks. Each helper validates what comes back and never lets it bypass a rule:

- ``read_problem``: facts from free text (physics, fluid, speed, size, purpose, quantities, material, load);
- ``rank_geometries``: picks templates from the real catalogue only (unknown keys are dropped);
- ``propose_parameters``: vary/keep and ranges, clipped to the template's validity limits (clipping is reported);
- ``free_geometry``: when no template fits, a CadQuery script that runs in a separate, isolated Python process
  with an import allow-list and a timeout; the solid then goes through the same CAD checks, marked as
  "language-model geometry, no published reference";
- ``review_mesh_plan``: suggestions on the rule-based plan; only y+ target, growth and layer count may change and
  only inside the rule limits; every accepted or refused change is written into the plan's rationale.

Every call can be logged with ``pinneapple_llm.ConversationStore`` for audit.

pinneapple_design.intent.package.doc:
DesignPackage: everything needed to build and check the design, and the files it is written to.

``package.write(folder)`` writes

* ``design.json`` - the whole package (requirements with provenance, plan, design, every number with its fidelity,
  margins, robustness, trust report, operating window, bill of materials, build instructions, limits);
* ``report.html`` (self-contained) and ``report.pdf``;
* ``geometry/<name>.stl``, ``.step`` (faceted B-rep), ``.glb``, and rendered views;
* ``check/`` - quantities of interest and a ``pinneapple-check.toml`` so that ``pinneapple check check/`` re-checks
  the margins and the agreement between the reduced model and the full solver.

pinneapple_design.intent.pipeline.doc:
RequirementSpec -> PhysicsPlan -> multi-fidelity optimisation -> verification and robustness.

The stages, each a plain function returning a typed object:

1. :func:`plan` - the template's physics, variables, models per fidelity (with availability) and failure modes;
2. :func:`optimise` - screen with the closed form (Latin hypercube), optimise with the design-study core
   (:mod:`pinneapple_design.design_optimizer`: NSGA-II on the reduced model, or the GP surrogate-assisted loop when the
   reduced model is slow), pick distinct candidates;
3. :func:`robustness` - Monte-Carlo propagation of the uncertain inputs (:func:`pinneapple_analysis.uncertainty.
   tolerance.propagate`) to the probability that every failure mode stays away, with a Wilson interval;
4. :func:`verify` - the full-fidelity solver on the chosen design, compared output by output with the reduced model;
5. :func:`assess` - the trust report (:class:`pinneapple_analysis.trust.TrustReport`) and the "will it work" verdict.

Every stage records the number of model calls and their wall time per fidelity.

pinneapple_design.intent.spec.doc:
Typed requirement objects: what the user asked for, with units, bounds and the provenance of every value.

A :class:`Quantity` is one number (always stored in SI) that remembers where it came from:

``stated``     the user wrote it (``quote`` is the text it was read from);
``answered``   the user gave it in reply to a follow-up question;
``defaulted``  the template filled it in (``source`` says from where: a standard, a datasheet, a scale preset);
``missing``    nobody gave it and it has no default: a critical one becomes a :class:`Question`.

A :class:`Slot` is a value a problem template can use (a load, a power, a temperature limit, ...), declared with its
SI unit, its admissible range and the words that point to it in a sentence. A :class:`RequirementSpec` is the filled
form: the template it was matched to, the values of its slots, the objectives and the open questions.

pinneapple_design.intent.template.doc:
Problem templates: how a kind of request maps to physics, design variables, models and failure modes.

A template is the unit a new domain plugs in with (see ``docs/guides/design_from_intent.md``, "Writing a template").
It declares

* the words that select it (``keywords``) and the values it can read from a request (``slots``);
* the governing physics and the assumptions behind it (``physics``, ``assumptions``);
* the design variables (``variables``), with discrete choices such as materials encoded as integer indices;
* the models at each fidelity (``fidelities``: closed form, reduced / surrogate, full solver) through
  ``evaluate(level, X, spec)``, which returns named outputs for a batch of designs;
* the failure modes, each a *margin* output that must be >= 0 (``failure_modes``);
* the uncertain inputs for the robustness study (``uncertainties``);
* the outputs of a design package: geometry, bill of materials, operating window, build instructions, limits.

Every number a template returns must come from a model, a cited datasheet or the user. Assumptions that are not
measured are listed in ``assumptions`` or ``limits`` so that the report shows them.

pinneapple_llm.qa.repair.doc:
Stage G: structured diagnosis and bounded automatic repair.

The loop is deliberately conservative about what "fixed" means:

* every attempt re-runs **all** validators, not just the one that failed;
* a candidate is accepted only when every validator the caller declared
  required is ``PASS`` -- compiling is not acceptance;
* the specification's criteria hash is snapshotted before the loop and
  re-checked after every attempt, so a "repair" that lowers a threshold,
  drops an invariant or changes reference data is rejected;
* an identical diff twice in a row means the loop is stuck and stops
  early instead of burning attempts;
* every attempt keeps its diagnosis, diff and findings, so a failed loop
  produces an actionable report rather than a shrug.

pinneapple_llm.workbench.store.doc:
The workbench database: a local SQLite file, or a PostgreSQL server you already run.

It keeps everything needed to resume, replay and later fine-tune:

- ``sessions``: settings and the lead's message history;
- ``events``: every step shown in the UI (messages, tool calls and results, approvals, plans, files);
- ``model_calls``: every request to the model with its exact input messages, the tool specs and the reply, the
  agent, timing and token counts. These are the fine-tuning examples (see :mod:`.finetune`);
- ``feedback``: your rating of a turn (+1 / -1) and an optional note, used to filter the training data.

``Store("~/.pinneapple/workbench/sessions.sqlite")`` or ``Store("postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/db")`` (needs
``pip install "psycopg[binary]"``). The same code and schema run on both.

pinneapple_llm.workbench.web.doc:
Internet search and page fetching for the agents.

Search providers, first configured wins:

- ``PINNEAPPLE_SEARXNG_URL``: your own SearXNG instance (private, no key; ``docker run -p 8888:8080 searxng/searxng``);
- ``BRAVE_API_KEY``: the Brave Search API;
- ``TAVILY_API_KEY``: the Tavily search API;
- otherwise DuckDuckGo's HTML endpoint (no key; may rate-limit heavy use).

The search query leaves your machine; the model and your files do not.

pinneapple_mcp.doc:
PINNeAPPle as an MCP server: solve, benchmark and validate physics problems from any coding agent.

    pip install "pinneapple[mcp]"
    pinneapple-mcp                       # stdio server; or: python -m pinneapple_mcp

The tools (``pinneapple_mcp.tools``) are plain functions with JSON inputs and outputs, usable without MCP:

* ``list_methods`` / ``describe_method``: solve methods and the method catalog with its validation status;
* ``list_presets``: problem presets;
* ``solve``: a problem (preset or JSON spec) by a method, with physics checks and a trust card (a job);
* ``trust_card``: physics checks of a field given inline or in a file (residual, boundary conditions,
  conservation, reference, applicability);
* ``benchmark``: several methods on one problem with the same training budget (a job);
* ``job_status`` / ``list_jobs`` / ``cancel_job``: long work runs as a job in a separate process;
* ``capabilities``: what this installation offers (optional APIs, limits, working directory).

Safe defaults (``pinneapple_mcp.config``): every result goes to a working directory and its paths are returned;
jobs run in a child process with a wall-time limit, a memory limit and the network switched off; files are read
only from allowed roots. ``import pinneapple_mcp`` does not import the MCP SDK.

pinneapple_physics.multiscale.comparison.doc:
Compare ensembles on a stream of multiscale queries: scale-blind against scale-aware selection, with and without
closure / consistency checks and scale-linking models.

* :func:`evaluate_experts`: run every expert once per query (its raw output on the common grid, with the filter it
  carries), time it, and run the label-free checks that need its internals (an LES's closure health on its own
  grid, a scale-conditioned model's cross-scale self-consistency); the table is cached in an ``.npz`` file;
* :func:`adapt_output`: bring a raw output to the requested filter, blindly (apply the requested filter on top of
  whatever the output already carries) or scale-aware (:func:`~.filters.refilter`, only what is realizable);
* :func:`raw_residual` / :func:`closed_multiscale_residual`: label-free residuals of a predicted pair of snapshots,
  at the grid scale or of the filtered equations with the subfilter term removed;
* :func:`run_ensembles`: one :class:`~pinneapple_physics.ensemble.PhysicsEnsemble` per configuration on the same
  stream, replaying the cached outputs;
* :func:`summarise_runs`, :func:`closure_flag_table`, :func:`markdown_tables`, :func:`summary_figure`.

pinneapple_physics.physics_audit.monitor.doc:
Physics validity monitor: is a prediction still physically plausible, when no ground truth is available?

A monitor holds a list of checks; each takes ``(prediction, query)`` and returns a number with a tolerance:

* :func:`residual_check`: norm of the governing-equation residual (from ``pp.compile``, a finite-difference stencil,
  the residual function of a :class:`~pinneapple_physics.ensemble.PhysicsEnsemble`...);
* :func:`conservation_check`: a conserved total (mass, energy, momentum) against its expected value;
* :func:`bounds_check`: admissible values (non-negative density or concentration, temperature above 0 K,
  probabilities in [0, 1]);
* :func:`boundary_check`: the prediction at the boundary against the boundary condition;
* :func:`constitutive_check`: any relation the fields must satisfy (equation of state, a material law);
* :func:`invariant_check`: a quantity that must not change from case to case or step to step (symmetry, energy of a
  conservative system);
* :func:`envelope_check`: the case's features against the model's training envelope (extrapolation).

The monitor keeps a history per check and raises the status from ``OK`` to ``WARN`` (a check failed) and ``ALARM``
(a critical check failed, or the same check failed ``persistence`` cases in a row, or a residual drifted to
``drift_ratio`` times its own baseline). Low residuals do not prove a prediction right (a model can satisfy its own
simplified equations and miss the real phenomenon) and a high residual …

pinneapple_query.refs.doc:
Stable physical references: where a quantity lives, independent of any mesh.

A reference names a *component* and a coordinate local to it, never a node or cell number, so it survives
remeshing, refinement and a change of method:

* ``"die@0.31,0.72"``: the die's junction surface at fractional in-plane coordinates (u, v) in [0, 1];
* ``"die@0.31,0.72,0.5"``: the same point at fractional height 0.5 through the die;
* ``"ubump[12,40]"``: the microbump of block (12, 40) (likewise ``tsv[...]``, ``c4[...]``);
* ``"interface:die/tim"``: the whole die/TIM interface; ``"interface:die/tim[3:9,0:4]"`` a block range of it;
* ``"region:die[3:9,0:4]"``: the junction surface over a block range;
* ``"lid:top"``: the cooled top face.

pinneapple_reason.report.doc:
Reports of a chain: ``chain.json``, ``report.md``, ``report.html`` (self-contained), ``trust.json`` and figures.

Figures (``figures/``):

* ``timeline.png``: version 0 -> version N as cards; each arrow names the failure found, the number that proves it
  and the countermeasure taken;
* ``margins.png``: every check (rows) on every version (columns), PASS / FAIL with demand / capacity;
* ``demand_capacity.png``: one panel per failure mode, demand and capacity across the versions;
* domain figures when the chain has the mode: minimum roof pitch by covering (``roof_min_slope.png``), natural
  frequency against the walking bands (``resonance.png``), head at the valve after closure (``water_hammer.png``).

pinneapple_reason.trust.doc:
Trust of a chain: the checks behind the final design, as a :class:`~pinneapple_analysis.trust.TrustReport` and as
the six-stage trust card of :func:`pinneapple_lab.curation.trust_stages`.

Checks gathered (kind used by the six-stage card in brackets):

* every requirement's failure mode on the final version, critical [decision: through the report's verdict];
* inputs recorded and finite [sanity -> data and geometry];
* the known-answer cases of every failure mode used [verification -> model];
* each computation's internal checks: energy balance closure, fixed-point convergence [physics];
* solver-against-closed-form agreements (finite-difference beam, Rayleigh frequency, MOC vs Joukowsky, ...)
  [reference -> benchmark];
* the worst margin when each uncertain premise moves by its declared spread, one at a time, and the applicability
  range of each model [uncertainty].

pinneapple_simulation.electromagnetics.a.doc:
Printed end-fire antennas (antipodal Vivaldi) with an external FEM solver: limits, checks, runner, surrogates.

* :mod:`.bounds` -- end-fire directivity limits (Hansen-Woodyard ``7.28 L/lambda``, ordinary ``4 L/lambda``),
  broadside aperture gain, the shortest length for a target gain and the feasibility frontier;
* :mod:`.checks` -- consistency checks of a result row as a ``TrustReport`` (realized gain, ``e = G/D``,
  ``e <= 1``, peak >= boresight, ``D`` below the end-fire limit, interpolated S11, far-field power closure);
* :mod:`.ava` -- an open parametric antipodal Vivaldi model for EMerge (``pip install emerge``) and its CLI;
* :mod:`.runner` -- one process per design and frequency with timeout, peak memory and the SuperLU fallback;
  the (design, frequency) table reader and writer;
* :mod:`.surrogates` -- frequency mean, HW + offset, linear physical features, Gaussian process; leave-one-design-
  out and held-out validation;
* :mod:`.design` -- DOE (LHS / Sobol), NSGA-II on the surrogates, active learning verified by the solver,
  multi-fidelity bias correction, a trust card per prediction;
* :mod:`.package` -- read an external study package (table + script) from a local folder and re-run a design;
* :mod:`.plots` -- directivity against ``L/lambda`` with the limits, LODO RMSE per model, layout.
* :mod:`.scene3d` -- the antenna in 3-D on the studio scene: copper and laminate (glTF / USD), the gain balloon per
  solved frequency, near-field cuts, self-contained viewers, …

pinneapple_simulation.electromagnetics.r.doc:
Reflector antennas and compact ranges by physical optics: geometry, PO currents and near fields, quiet zones.

* :mod:`.geometry` -- offset paraboloid sections (superellipse junction) with blended rolled edges, meshed;
* :mod:`.po` -- ``cos^q`` feeds, plane waves, PO currents, the exact radiated near field;
* :mod:`.analytic` -- closed forms that check them (PO disk on axis, GO aperture taper);
* :mod:`.qz` -- quiet-zone metrics (taper, ripple, phase, usable size);
* :mod:`.compact_range` -- a compact range (feed gain, section size, rolled edge) and its sweeps.

Guide: docs/guides/compact_range.md.

Quiet-zone metrics of a collimated field (a compact range, a plane-wave synthesiser, a near-field probe scan).

:func:`qz_metrics` takes the complex co-polar field on points of a plane transverse to the range axis and returns,
over a disk of the quiet-zone diameter:

* ``amp_pp_dB`` / ``phase_pp_deg`` -- peak-to-peak amplitude (dB) and phase (deg) variation;
* ``taper_dB`` -- the range of a quadratic surface fitted to the amplitude in dB (the smooth illumination taper);
* ``ripple_pp_dB`` -- peak-to-peak residual about that quadratic (the ripple, mostly edge diffraction);
* ``phase_ripple_pp_deg`` -- the same for the phase (deg) about a fitted plane plus quadratic;
* ``usable_diameter`` -- the largest centred disk where the amplitude stays within ``+-amp_tol`` dB and the phase
  within ``+-phase_tol`` deg (peak-to-peak 2 amp_tol and 2 phase_tol), searched over the points given (give a
  region larger than the quiet zone to find it when it is larger);
* ``xpol_dB`` -- the largest cross-polar to co-polar ratio in the disk, when the cross-polar field is given.

pinneapple_simulation.electromagnetics.w.doc:
Closed-form wave electromagnetics, ``exp(+j omega t)`` convention (see :mod:`.media`).

* plane waves at interfaces: :func:`fresnel`, :func:`brewster_angle`, :func:`critical_angle`, and the
  characteristic-matrix method for planar multilayers :func:`multilayer` (Born & Wolf, *Principles of Optics*,
  7th ed., sec. 1.5-1.6);
* conductors: :func:`skin_depth`, :func:`surface_impedance`;
* guided waves and resonators: rectangular and circular waveguide cut-offs and fields, rectangular and
  cylindrical cavity resonances and the conductor-loss Q of the rectangular TE101 mode (D. M. Pozar,
  *Microwave Engineering*, 4th ed., ch. 3 and 6; Jackson ch. 8);
* radiation: the Hertzian dipole's complete fields and power, the thin centre-fed dipole pattern of any length,
  the half-wave dipole's radiation resistance and directivity (C. A. Balanis, *Antenna Theory*, 4th ed., ch. 4);
* the exact TM (E along the axis) scattering of a plane wave by a dielectric or perfectly conducting circular
  cylinder as a series of cylinder functions (R. F. Harrington, *Time-Harmonic Electromagnetic Fields*, 1961,
  sec. 5-8 and 5-9; C. A. Balanis, *Advanced Engineering Electromagnetics*, 2nd ed., sec. 11.5);
* surface plasmon polaritons on a flat metal-dielectric interface (S. A. Maier, *Plasmonics*, 2007, ch. 2)
  and the bulk plasma dispersion.

pinneapple_simulation.flight.trim.doc:
Trim, ground equilibrium and stability derivatives of an :class:`~.aircraft.Aircraft`.

* :func:`trim`: steady straight flight at true airspeed V, altitude h and flight-path angle gamma: angle of attack,
  elevator and throttle that zero the body accelerations (u_dot, w_dot, q_dot), wings level, no sideslip;
* :func:`ground_equilibrium`: height and pitch attitude at rest on the gear (engines idle);
* :func:`stability_derivatives`: the usual non-dimensional derivatives by central differences of the full model about
  a trimmed state (rates non-dimensionalised with b / 2V and c / 2V), and the static margin -Cm_alpha / CL_alpha.

pinneapple_simulation.flight.verificatio.doc:
Verification of the flight-dynamics building blocks, as numbers a guide or a CI job can quote.

    from pinneapple_simulation.flight import verification
    rep = verification.run()                 # dict: turbulence, trim, conservation, lattice

* turbulence: variance and band-averaged PSD ratio (generated / target) of each generator and component;
* trim: trim residual and the drift of airspeed, altitude and load factor over 10 s stick fixed, every aircraft;
* conservation: energy and angular-momentum error of the integrator for a spinning body under gravity;
* lattice: lift of the lattice-in-the-loop model against the steady vortex lattice at two angles.

pinneapple_simulation.numerical_solvers..doc:
3-D lattice Boltzmann wind tunnel in PyTorch, with the memory and boundary techniques FluidX3D made popular.

Written from the published methods (no FluidX3D code is used):

- velocity sets D3Q19 and D3Q27; SRT (BGK) or TRT collision (magic parameter 3/16);
- Smagorinsky-Lilly subgrid model for high Reynolds numbers (Hou et al. 1994);
- **DDF shifting**: the distributions are stored as g_i = f_i - w_i, so the stored numbers are small deviations and keep
  their precision (Lehmann et al. 2022, "Accuracy and performance of the lattice Boltzmann method with 64-bit, 32-bit,
  and customized 16-bit number formats");
- **16-bit storage** ``storage="fp16s"``: the shifted distributions are kept in IEEE half precision, scaled so they stay
  in the normal range; all arithmetic is still FP32. Memory per cell drops from 4q to 2q bytes for the distributions;
- equilibrium boundaries for inflow and open sides (cells fixed to the equilibrium of a given density and velocity),
  a zero-gradient outflow, no-slip walls, a moving floor and moving solids (bounce-back with the wall-velocity term);
- a global volume force (Guo forcing), for pressure-driven ducts;
- the force on a solid by momentum exchange on its boundary links;
- STL voxelization (trimesh) and unit conversion between SI and lattice units;
- VTK export of the fields in the legacy structured-points layout FluidX3D also writes, so its output and this one are
  read by the same function (:func:`read_vtk`).

    from …

Operator-learning benchmark datasets: 1-D Burgers, 2-D Darcy, 2-D Navier-Stokes (vorticity).

The three problems and input distributions of Li et al. (2021), "Fourier Neural Operator for Parametric Partial
Differential Equations" (ICLR), solved with the reference solvers of :mod:`.operator_benchmarks`. This module holds the
dataset recipes of the zoo datasets ``burgers_1d_li2021``, ``darcy_2d_li2021`` and
``navier_stokes_2d_li2021_nu1e-3`` (``pp.datasets``):

* :data:`BURGERS`, :data:`DARCY`, :data:`NS`: the problem parameters, resolutions, time steps, seeds and split sizes;
* ``burgers_inputs`` / ``darcy_inputs`` / ``ns_inputs`` and ``solve_burgers`` / ``solve_darcy`` / ``solve_ns``;
* :func:`convergence`: the resolution / time-step study each dataset card quotes;
* :func:`generate`: writes ``<split>[-NN].npz`` files and ``meta.json`` (``scripts/zoo/operator_data.py`` is the CLI).

Every input is drawn in the parent process from a fixed seed, so the data do not depend on the number of workers.

pinneapple_simulation.pipelines.freecad_.doc:
Post-processing of a finished internal-flow case: what the pipeline reports and checks.

Everything is read from the case (latest time) with the finite-volume reader of :mod:`pinneapple_check`
(``load_openfoam``, :class:`~pinneapple_check.fv.FV`) and from the solver log:

* per inlet / outlet patch: area, area-averaged static pressure (Pa), volume flux (m³/s, from ``phi``), and the
  bulk (flux-weighted, mixing-cup) value of the passive scalar;
* pressure drop inlet -> outlet (static and total), mass imbalance |sum of boundary fluxes| / inflow;
* cross sections (:class:`Section`): discs sampled inside the fluid, area-averaged pressure, flux through them and
  bulk scalar (e.g. the pressure gradient inside a bend, a bulk temperature along a heated pipe);
* forces on the walls and y+ per wall patch (:func:`wall_loads`, from the fields), the largest Courant number of a
  transient run (solver log);
* figures: speed and pressure on a plane through the domain, the mesh cut by that plane.

pinneapple_systems.sports.football.pc_st.doc:
Pass outcome: physics-based pitch control against data-only classifiers at equal data.

Train on one Metrica match, test on the other (and the swap). Every model sees the same passes:

* ``pc_default`` -- Spearman (2018) pitch control with the parameters common in open implementations (no fit);
* ``pc_reaction`` / ``pc_accel`` -- the same with the reaction-time or the acceleration-limited time to intercept,
  parameters fitted by maximum likelihood on the training passes (5 numbers);
* ``logistic`` / ``gbm`` -- logistic regression and gradient-boosted trees on geometric features
  (:func:`pitch_control.geometric_features`), no physics;
* ``hybrid`` -- the trees with the pitch-control probability and the intercept-time margin as extra features.

:func:`data_efficiency` repeats the comparison with 25 to all training passes (random subsets, several seeds).

pinneapple_systems.time_series.enso.data.doc:
The ENSO study data set: monthly indices and maps built from the cached sources of :mod:`.data`.

:func:`build_dataset` returns an :class:`EnsoDataset` with, from ``start`` to ``end``:

* Nino 3.4 / 3 / 4 / 1+2 SST anomalies [K] from OISST v2.1 (box means of 1 deg monthly means);
* SST anomaly maps on the intermediate model's grid (default 2 deg x 1 deg, 120E-80W, 20S-20N);
* D20 anomalies [m] in 10 deg longitude bins (130E-80W) for 5S-5N and 2S-2N, gaps filled month by month from the
  available bins with the leading EOFs of the base period (:func:`eof_fill`; no month uses another month's data);
  the warm-water-volume proxy ``wwv`` is their zonal mean over 5S-5N, ``h_west`` / ``h_east`` the means west / east
  of 155W (the tilt mode);
* ERA5 zonal wind stress anomalies on the model grid and a Tahiti - Darwin pressure index (1959-2022 only; NaN
  after 2022-12).

All anomalies are taken from calendar-month climatologies of the ``base`` period -- choose it inside the
development period so the test period does not inform them. ``checks`` holds the cross-checks against independent
sources (statsmodels Nino 1+2, NCEI heat content, ERA5 SST).

pinneapple_tools.benchmark_suite.crossfr.doc:
Track B of the cross-framework benchmark: the Darcy data, its normalisation and the one training loop.

The three FNO implementations (PINNeAPPle, PhysicsNeMo, neuraloperator) are trained by the same loop on the same
data in the same order, so the comparison measures the model implementations and their speed, not three different
trainers. Protocol (``benchmarks/vs_frameworks/PROTOCOL.md``, track B):

* data: zoo dataset ``darcy_2d_li2021`` (Li et al. 2021 problem, 128 x 128), cell-averaged to 64 x 64
  (:func:`pinneapple_simulation.numerical_solvers.operator_datasets.block_average`);
* split: train rows 0-899, validation 900-999, the 200 test rows;
* normalisation: input scaled by the training mean / std (scalars), output per-pixel unit Gaussian from the training
  rows, decoded before the loss;
* loss: per-sample relative L2 averaged over the batch; Adam with weight decay, cosine schedule over all steps;
  batch 20; a fixed number of epochs; the data order of epoch e is ``default_rng(seed * 1000 + e).permutation``.

pinneapple_tools.visualization.integrati.doc:
Rerun (rerun.io) bridge: stream PINNeAPPle results into the Rerun viewer, live or into an ``.rrd`` file.

Rerun is a time-aware multimodal viewer: 3-D scenes, point clouds, meshes, time series, text and graphs on shared
timelines, scrubbable after the fact. That fits digital twins and simulations, where a field, a sensor reading, the
active model and a diagnosis all change with time. This bridge logs:

* :meth:`RerunLogger.scene`: a ``studio.Scene`` (surfaces coloured by a field, streamlines);
* :meth:`RerunLogger.points` / :meth:`RerunLogger.mesh`: raw point clouds and triangle meshes;
* :meth:`RerunLogger.molecule`: a molecule (and its metal slab) from :mod:`..molecular`, frame by frame;
* :meth:`RerunLogger.scalars`: time series (sensor values, errors, weights of an ensemble);
* :meth:`RerunLogger.ensemble_run`: a :class:`~pinneapple_physics.ensemble.EnsembleRun` (weights, errors, active
  model per case);
* :meth:`RerunLogger.graph`: a knowledge graph or a proof graph as a graph view, nodes coloured by status;
* :meth:`RerunLogger.text`: a Markdown report (physics audit, proof report).

    from pinneapple_tools.visualization.integrations.rerun_bridge import RerunLogger
    log = RerunLogger("pinneapple", path="run.rrd")          # or spawn=True for a live viewer
    log.molecule(mol, frames, times, slab=slab)
    log.close()                                              # then: rerun run.rrd

Needs ``pip install rerun-sdk``.
